//? Title: Serve a Python-trained TensorFlow SavedModel from C++
//? Subtitle: Resolve a named serving signature and test dynamic batches
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Load the upstream Python-trained affine model with Datamunge's optional
TensorFlow backend. Resolve the serving signature, check dynamic batch
inference, and compare predictions with exported references. A native
weight-only example shows how to deploy the same affine computation using NPZ.
)
\toc

> Load and serve the SavedModel

The bundled fixture comes from `scripts/train_export_affine.py` in the
TensorFlow examples checkout. It trained on 128 sinusoidal feature pairs
with 600 gradient updates. The export stores the serving graph, variables,
and a SignatureDef mapping logical keys to tensor endpoints. Datamunge
loads the serve-tagged graph and restores its checkpoint using the
TensorFlow C++ runtime. The inference executable needs no Python runtime.

Build Datamunge with `-DDATAMUNGE_ENABLE_TENSORFLOW=ON` and point
`CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` at a TensorFlow C++ SDK containing its headers and
libraries. On a build with the backend disabled, this block reports its
availability. The native weight-only exercise below still runs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)", id=chunk-01, cache=yes}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <fstream>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  if (!n::saved_model_runtime_available()) {
    std::println("SavedModel requires a Datamunge build with DATAMUNGE_ENABLE_TENSORFLOW.");
    return 0;
  }
  const std::string fixture = "fixtures/python_affine/";
  const n::SavedModel model(fixture + "saved_model");
  if (model.input_shape() != n::Shape{-1, 2} || model.output_shape() != n::Shape{-1, 1})
    throw std::runtime_error("Unexpected serving signature");
  std::ifstream rows(fixture + "reference.tsv");
  std::vector<float> input, reference;
  float a, b, y;
  while (rows >> a >> b >> y) {
    input.insert(input.end(), {a, b});
    reference.push_back(y);
  }
  if (reference.size() != 4) throw std::runtime_error("Missing reference predictions");
  const auto prediction = model.predict(n::from_vector(input, {4, 2}));
  const auto single = model.predict(n::tensor({-2., 4.}, {1, 2}));
  if (prediction.shape() != n::Shape{4, 1} || single.shape() != n::Shape{1, 1} ||
      prediction.requires_grad() || std::abs(single.item() + 7.25) > 1e-5)
    throw std::runtime_error("Unexpected inference result");
  const auto actual = prediction.to_vector<float>();
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < actual.size(); ++i)
    if (std::abs(actual[i] - reference[i]) > 1e-6f)
      throw std::runtime_error("SavedModel output differs from Python reference");
  bool rejected = false;
  try {
    (void)model.predict(n::zeros({1, 3}));
  } catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {
    rejected = true;
  }
  if (!rejected) throw std::runtime_error("Incompatible feature width was accepted");
  std::println("Serving signature features [-1, 2] to predictions [-1, 1].");
  std::println("Four predictions {:.6f}, {:.6f}, {:.6f}, {:.6f}. Single-row prediction {:.6f}.",
               actual[0], actual[1], actual[2], actual[3], single.item());
  std::println("Incompatible feature width rejected.");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Serving signature features [-1, 2] to predictions [-1, 1].
Four predictions 0.250000, 0.999999, -1.249999, 6.249995. Single-row prediction -7.249994.
Incompatible feature width rejected.
// result_end

> The method

Serving separates parameter estimation from evaluation. Once weights W and b are fixed,
Y=XW+b has shape [batch,output_width], and batch may vary between calls. The native helper
requires floating inputs, weights and bias of the same dtype and compatible dimensions.
The fixture fits the full-rank design Z=[1,X] by least squares:

$$
\widehat\theta=\arg\min_\theta\|Z\theta-y\|_2^2,\qquad y=.75x_0-1.5x_1+.25.
$$
\alttext(Estimate an intercept and two slopes from a full-rank training design.)

Native NPZ serialization exports learned coefficients, not executable graph functions or SignatureDefs. The C++
consumer retains the affine computation and performs explicit shape/dtype validation. NoGradGuard
inside affine_inference avoids retaining an inference graph. Dense evaluation costs
O(batch times input_width times output_width). Matching outputs on a few queries is a useful
contract check, not proof of generalization to another population.

The concept diagram below is drawn by `dm::graphics::Diagram`. Its editable C++
source is the complete block below; regeneration uses the same datamunge toolchain
as the numerical examples. Exports consume the saved local SVG.

```{cpp, file="figures/saved_model_inference_concept.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram;
  diagram.node("n0", {0.0, 0}, "Fit in Native C++\nleast squares coefficients");
  diagram.node("n1", {5.2, 0}, "Export Float32 NPZ\nweights + bias");
  diagram.node("n2", {10.4, 0}, "C++ serving contract\ndynamic batch; no grad");
  diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
  diagram.save_svg("build/saved_model_inference_concept.svg");
}
```
